{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>You shall not muzzle the ox.</b> Scripture spoke of the present case, and the same applies to every beast, wild animal, and bird that work at a task involving food. If so, why is 'ox' stated? To exclude a person (Sifrei; Bava Metzia ch. 8)." "<b>While it threshes.</b> One might think he may muzzle it from outside? Scripture says: לֹא תַחְסֹם שׁוֹר — in any case. And why is 'threshing' stated? To tell you: just as threshing is distinct in that it is a thing whose work and growth are not completed from the earth, so too anything like it [is included] — a milker, a cheesemaker, and a churner, whose [product's] growth is not from the earth, are excluded; a kneader and one who presses out [the dough into loaves], whose work is completed for bread, are excluded; one who separates dates and dried figs, whose work is completed for tithing, are excluded (Sifrei; Bava Metzia ch. 9)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {1 "The logic: threshing produces food still attached to its source (not yet 'completed from the earth'), so the prohibition applies; the excluded cases involve food whose processing is already complete — the verse covers only work on uncompleted produce."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:48:21.156832616Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}